(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-cam-nutrition-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"CAM/Nutrition Exam 1 — Master Exam (Form B)","category":"CAM / Nutrition","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Per the ACOG recommendations cited in the lecture, what is the recommended folic acid dose once a woman of average risk is already pregnant?","choices":["0.4 mg","4 mg","0.6 mg","2.6 mcg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states ACOG recommends 0.6 mg of folic acid during pregnancy. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"The lecture lists thickened-liquid consistencies for dysphagia diet orders. Which set matches?","choices":["Diluted, watery, and thin","Nectar thick, honey thick, and pudding thick","Clear, sparkling, and lightly carbonated","Frozen, boiled, and steamed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the thickened-liquid orders listed are nectar thick, honey thick, and pudding thick. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is the average daily calorie need range given for men, per lecture?","choices":["500 to 800 kcal","2,400 to 3,200 kcal","1,200 to 1,600 kcal","1,800 to 2,400 kcal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Average daily calorie need for men is 2,400 to 3,200 kcal per day, per lecture. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"How does orlistat work as a weight-loss medication, per lecture?","choices":["It slows gastric emptying by roughly 30% and directly enhances feelings of satiety.","It blocks approximately 30% of carbohydrate absorption occurring in the colon.","It suppresses appetite by acting directly on hunger centers in the brain.","It reduces fat digestion by about 30% by inhibiting the lipase enzyme in the small intestine."],"answer":3,"exp":"Orlistat reduces fat digestion by about 30% by inhibiting lipase enzyme action in the small intestine. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"The lecture characterizes the Standard American Diet as high in which combination?","choices":["Unsaturated oils and legumes","Animal fat, simple carbohydrates, and sodium","Fiber, potassium, and plant-based foods","Complex carbohydrates and lean protein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the Standard American Diet is described as high in animal fat, simple carbohydrates, and sodium. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What distinguishes nonceliac gluten sensitivity from celiac disease, according to the lecture?","choices":["Gluten sensitivity requires lifelong avoidance of dairy, while celiac disease does not.","Gluten sensitivity produces intestinal damage identical to celiac disease.","Gluten sensitivity causes symptoms after gluten ingestion but no intestinal damage.","Gluten sensitivity is unrelated to any dietary trigger."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states nonceliac gluten sensitivity involves experiencing 'symptoms after ingestion of gluten, but no intestinal damage,' unlike celiac disease, which flattens villi. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which of the following is a contraindication for enteral nutrition (and thus an indication for parenteral nutrition instead)?","choices":["Severe ileus or feeding intolerance","Recent uncomplicated dental work","Mild nausea after meals","A functional GI tract with good appetite"],"answer":0,"exp":"Severe ileus or feeding intolerance is a listed contraindication for enteral nutrition (along with intestinal obstruction, hemodynamic instability, severe malabsorption, major GI bleed, and major GI surgery requiring gut rest). — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"The lecture identifies which group as the fastest-growing segment of the population, projected to reach 19 million by 2050?","choices":["Adolescents completing physical growth","Adults aged 30 to 40 years","People aged 85 years and older","People aged 65 to 74 years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: adults 85 and older are described as the fastest-growing segment, projected to reach 19 million by 2050. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"According to the lecture, which of the following best defines a food allergy?","choices":["An adverse reaction to food that involves an immune response","An adverse reaction to food that does not involve an allergic reaction","A delayed reaction to food that only affects the GI tract","A permanent flattening of intestinal villi caused by gluten exposure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines food allergy exactly this way, distinguishing it from food intolerance. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"The lecture highlights tomatoes as a source of which phytochemical, used as an example of a functional food?","choices":["Anthocyanin","Allicin","Lycopene","Beta-carotene"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture specifically names lycopene as the phytochemical in tomatoes that is important for health, used to introduce the concept of functional foods. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 45 (Functional Foods)"},{"q":"In the lecture's list of allergic reaction symptoms by body system, which cardiovascular findings are both marked with an asterisk as signs of rapid, potentially fatal anaphylaxis?","choices":["Itching and hives appearing across the skin","Nausea and vomiting in the GI tract","Low blood pressure and rapid heart rate (cardiovascular)","Runny nose and wheezing in the airway"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lecture asterisks both low blood pressure and rapid heart rate under the cardiovascular system as indicators of rapid, potentially fatal anaphylaxis. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A fatty acid with more than two double bonds in its carbon chain is classified as:","choices":["Monounsaturated","Saturated","Trans","Polyunsaturated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Polyunsaturated fatty acids (like omega-3 alpha-linolenic acid and omega-6 linoleic acid) have more than two double bonds in the carbon chain. — Macro/Micro lecture, slides 50-53 (Saturated, Monounsaturated & Polyunsaturated Fatty Acid Structure)"},{"q":"According to the lecture, whole milk is recommended for its extra fat energy up to what age, after which reduced-fat or fat-free is preferred?","choices":["Up to 6 months of age","Up to 5 years of age","Up to 10 years of age","Up to 2 years of age"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: whole milk is recommended up to 2 years for extra fat energy, after which reduced-fat or fat-free is preferred. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 320"},{"q":"Which mineral is specifically named, along with calcium and magnesium, as emphasized in the DASH diet to help lower blood pressure?","choices":["Iron mineral","Potassium","Copper trace","Fluoride"],"answer":1,"exp":"The DASH diet is high in calcium, potassium, and magnesium, and low in fat and sodium, with an emphasis on fruits and vegetables (naturally low-salt, high-potassium foods) and low-fat dairy. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 158 (Dietary Approach to Stop Hypertension)"},{"q":"According to the lecture, roughly what share of youth are overweight at the time they are diagnosed with type 2 diabetes?","choices":["About 40%","About 100%","About 85%","About 10%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: about 85% of youth are overweight at diagnosis of type 2 diabetes. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 470"},{"q":"When first spoon-feeding an infant, the lecture says to expect the infant to take about how much?","choices":["Nothing at all for the first month","Only 2 or 3 bites","A full jar of baby food","At least 1 cup of puree"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: expect an infant to take only 2 or 3 bites at first meals. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 255"},{"q":"During a single breastfeeding session, milk released 10 to 20 minutes into the feeding is called hindmilk. How does it differ from foremilk according to the lecture?","choices":["Hindmilk is released within the first 3 to 5 minutes of a feed and closely resembles skim milk.","Hindmilk contains noticeably more water and less fat content than foremilk.","Hindmilk resembles cream, reflecting its higher fat content compared to the more water-like foremilk.","Hindmilk is only present within colostrum, not within mature milk."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states hindmilk, released after 10-20 minutes into feeding, resembles cream, while foremilk (released in the first 3-5 minutes) resembles skim milk. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What BMI criteria qualify a patient for bariatric surgery, per lecture?","choices":["BMI ≥50 alone, with no lower comorbidity-based threshold","BMI ≥40, or BMI ≥35 with obesity-related comorbid conditions","BMI ≥25 alone, regardless of comorbid conditions","BMI ≥30 alone, without any comorbidity requirement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Bariatric surgery candidates generally have a BMI ≥40, or a BMI ≥35 with obesity-related comorbid conditions. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What BMI range is described in lecture as usually incompatible with life?","choices":["18.5–20","20–22","11–13","15–17"],"answer":2,"exp":"A BMI of 11–13 is usually incompatible with life, per lecture. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which micronutrients are emphasized for wound healing in trauma patients, per lecture?","choices":["Vitamin C, vitamin A, and zinc","Vitamin K and magnesium","Vitamin D, calcium, and phosphorus","Vitamin B12, folate, and iron"],"answer":0,"exp":"Vitamin C, vitamin A, and zinc are emphasized to support wound healing in trauma patients (consistent with the same micronutrient trio flagged for pressure wounds). — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"According to the lecture, what is the actual difference between a 'calorie' (lowercase c) and a 'kilocalorie' (kcal)?","choices":["A calorie measures only the energy content of fat, while a kcal measures only carbohydrate energy","They are different names for the exact same amount of heat energy, used interchangeably in lecture and on food labels","A calorie raises the temperature of 1 g of water 1°C, while a kcal raises 1,000 g (1 L) of water 1°C","A kcal is 1/1000 the size of a calorie, making the calorie the larger of the two heat-energy units used in lecture"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture defines calorie (little c) as the heat needed to raise 1 g of water 1°C, and kilocalorie as the heat needed to raise 1,000 g (1 L) of water 1°C — 1 kcal equals 1,000 calories. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 22 (Calories Defined)"},{"q":"A newborn's fetal weight is documented at the 5th percentile for gestational age. What term does the lecture use for this classification?","choices":["Classified as macrosomic","Termed low birth weight","Small for gestational age (SGA)","Considered a post-term birth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines SGA as fetal weight less than the 10th percentile for gestational age, which matches a fetus at the 5th percentile. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"About how many kcal does 1 pound of weight loss represent, per lecture?","choices":["500 kcal","1,000 kcal","7,000 kcal","3,500 kcal"],"answer":3,"exp":"1 pound of weight loss is approximately 3,500 kcal, per lecture. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How is BMI calculated, per the formula given in lecture?","choices":["Weight in kg divided by height in meters squared","Weight in kg multiplied by height in meters","Weight in lbs divided by height in inches","Height in cm divided by weight in kg"],"answer":0,"exp":"BMI = weight in kilograms divided by height in meters squared, per the formula given (with conversions: lbs/2.2 = kg, inches × 2.54 = cm). — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How does the lecture define a monounsaturated fatty acid?","choices":["A fatty acid that contains no carbon","A fatty acid with one double bond, such as oleic acid (an omega-9)","A fatty acid with only single carbon bonds","A fatty acid with more than two double bonds"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: a monounsaturated fatty acid has one double bond, exemplified by oleic acid (omega-9). — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 51"},{"q":"On physical exam, a pregnant patient has enlarged parotid glands and eroded tooth enamel. Per the lecture, these findings are associated with which eating disorder?","choices":["Bulimia","Anorexia","Binge eating disorder","Pica"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture lists enlarged parotid glands and eroded tooth enamel as physical exam signs associated with bulimia. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"To be classified as an \"essential nutrient,\" a substance must meet which set of criteria per lecture?","choices":["It must provide at least 4 kcal per gram, be required in gram quantities daily, and be classified separately from micronutrients.","A specific biological function must be identified, omitting it from the diet causes a decline in function, and replacing it restores normal function.","It must be synthesized entirely by the body, never require dietary intake, and remain unaffected by omission from the diet.","It must appear on the Nutrition Facts label, list a Percent Daily Value, and meet FDA disclosure requirements for that nutrient."],"answer":1,"exp":"Per lecture, an essential nutrient must have one specific biological function identified, its omission from the diet must cause a decline in that function, and replacing it in the diet must restore normal function. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 5 (Nutrition — Essential Nutrient)"},{"q":"A patient weighs 176 pounds. Using the lecture's conversion (divide pounds by 2.2), approximately how many kilograms is this?","choices":["40 kg","176 kg","387 kg","80 kg"],"answer":3,"exp":"176 lb ÷ 2.2 = 80 kg, following the lecture's stated conversion method exactly. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 26 (Metric System Summary (1))"},{"q":"According to the lecture, which combination correctly pairs a cause of peptic ulcers with its corresponding treatment?","choices":["Overuse of NSAIDs; treated with laxatives","H. pylori infection; treated with a low-FODMAP diet","H. pylori infection; treated with antibiotics","Acid secreted by the stomach; treated with increased fiber intake"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists H. pylori infection as a cause of ulcers and antibiotics as the treatment to eradicate it. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"In the Blue Zones dietary pattern, what is described as the main dietary fat?","choices":["Coconut oil","Lard","Olive oil","Butter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: olive oil is identified as the main dietary fat in the Blue Zones pattern. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as a malnutrition risk factor that develops AFTER hospital admission, rather than before it?","choices":["Long-term nursing home placement","Longstanding preexisting chronic illness","Chronic household food insecurity","NPO/fasting status for procedures"],"answer":3,"exp":"NPO/fasting for procedures is listed among the \"after admit\" risk factors, along with acute illness, worsening chronic illness, need for mealtime assistance, and meal restrictions. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Per the prevalence data cited, what percentage of hospitalized adults are actually diagnosed with malnutrition (as opposed to merely being at risk or declining)?","choices":["40% to 70%","20% to 50%","Over 90%","5% to 8%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Only 5% to 8% of hospitalized adults are actually diagnosed with malnutrition, a smaller figure than the 20-50% estimated to BE malnourished or the 10-65% who experience a nutrition decline. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"According to the lecture, how are vitamins classified, and which type does cooking destroy more readily?","choices":["Organic and inorganic; cooking destroys inorganic vitamins more readily","Fat-soluble (A, D, E, K) and water-soluble (B vitamins and C); cooking destroys water-soluble vitamins more readily","Major and trace; cooking destroys trace vitamins more readily","Simple and complex; cooking destroys complex vitamins more readily"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: vitamins are fat-soluble (A, D, E, K) or water-soluble (B and C), and cooking destroys water-soluble ones more readily. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 18"},{"q":"A sandwich contains 46 grams of carbohydrate, 14 grams of fat, 45 grams of protein, and 0 grams of alcohol. Using 4/9/4/7 kcal per gram, approximately how many total kilocalories does it provide?","choices":["550 kcal","360 kcal","490 kcal","405 kcal"],"answer":2,"exp":"184 kcal from carbohydrate (46 × 4) + 126 kcal from fat (14 × 9) + 180 kcal from protein (45 × 4) + 0 kcal from alcohol = 490 kcal total, matching the lecture's grilled chicken sandwich example. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 23 (Calculating Calories)"},{"q":"Which liver-disease feeding route carries higher risk of complications from ascites, varices, and bleeding, per lecture?","choices":["NGT/NJT","PEG/PEJ/GT","PO (oral) intake","Parenteral nutrition carries no additional risk in this setting."],"answer":1,"exp":"PEG/PEJ/GT placement carries a higher risk of complications from ascites, varices, and bleeding in chronic liver disease. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What health risks are specifically associated with upper-body (android, \"apple-shaped\") fat distribution, per lecture?","choices":["No documented health risks whatsoever when compared to lower-body, gynoid fat distribution patterns.","Insulin resistance and fatty liver, with increased risk of cancer, type 2 diabetes, high blood lipids, and heart disease","A measurably reduced risk of developing type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome, and cardiovascular disease.","Improved whole-body insulin sensitivity, more favorable blood lipids, and lower cardiovascular risk."],"answer":1,"exp":"Android (upper-body/apple-shaped) obesity is associated with insulin resistance and fatty liver, and leads to obesity-related chronic diseases including cancer, type 2 diabetes, high blood lipids, and heart disease. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What does direct calorimetry measure, per lecture?","choices":["Oxygen intake and carbon dioxide output","Body heat output, using an insulated chamber","Body fat percentage via electrical resistance","Blood glucose response to a meal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Direct calorimetry measures body heat output using an insulated chamber — it's accurate but expensive and complex. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"When introducing solid foods, the lecture recommends waiting how long before introducing a new food to check for intolerance?","choices":["About 30 days","No waiting is needed","About 7 days","About 1 hour"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: introduce one food at a time and wait about 7 days before adding a new food to check for intolerance. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 243"},{"q":"Which eating-behavior tactic is recommended during meals, per lecture?","choices":["Put the fork down between bites and chew thoroughly and slowly.","Always finish every item on the plate.","Eat as quickly as possible to reduce time spent thinking about food.","Eat while distracted by texting or watching television to reduce boredom."],"answer":0,"exp":"Putting the fork down between bites, chewing thoroughly and slowly, leaving some food on the plate, and avoiding distraction while eating are recommended eating-behavior tactics. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"The lecture cites an American Academy of Pediatrics recommendation to screen children's cholesterol at which ages?","choices":["Between ages 9 and 11","At birth","Only after age 18","Between ages 1 and 2"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the lecture cites cholesterol screening between ages 9 and 11. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 456"},{"q":"What is GERD, as described in the lecture?","choices":["A mild, one-time episode of acid reflux","A recurrent, serious form of heartburn/acid reflux","A type of peptic ulcer caused by NSAID overuse","An allergic reaction causing wheezing and swelling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes GERD as a recurrent, serious form of heartburn. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed in the lecture as a consequence of maternal obesity during the preconception and pregnancy period?","choices":["Increased fertility and likelihood of spontaneous conception.","Decreased risk of cesarean delivery.","Increased risk of hypertensive disorders of pregnancy.","Decreased postpartum weight retention."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture explicitly lists increased risk of hypertensive disorders of pregnancy among the preconception/pregnancy effects of obesity. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"The lecture advises that supplemental water is NOT recommended during which period, even in hot climates?","choices":["Only for formula-fed infants","During the first 6 months","During the first 2 years","Only after solid foods begin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: supplemental water is not recommended during the first 6 months, even in hot climates. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 156"},{"q":"Which type of omega-3 fatty acid does the lecture identify as plant-based, in contrast to the fish-oil-derived types?","choices":["Docosahexaenoic acid (DHA)","Eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA)","Arachidonic acid","Alpha-linolenic acid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture identifies alpha-linolenic acid as the plant-based omega-3 fatty acid. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"The lecture states that once adolescence and physical growth are complete, the body's use of nutrients shifts primarily toward which purpose?","choices":["Rapid growth and tissue expansion","Storage of excess fat for later growth","Building peak bone mass","Maintenance rather than growth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: in adulthood nutrients switch to maintenance instead of growth. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A patient eats whole grains, vegetables, fruits, legumes, and seaweed, and limits animal foods to white meat or white-meat fish once or twice a week. Which diet type does this describe?","choices":["Macrobiotic","Pescatarian","Semi-vegetarian","Lacto-ovovegetarian"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines the macrobiotic diet as whole grains, vegetables, fruits, legumes, and seaweeds, with animal foods limited to white meat or white-meat fish once or twice a week. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"How does the lecture contrast the two forms of digestible starch?","choices":["Amylose is highly branched and amylopectin is straight","Both are forms of nondigestible dietary fiber","Both are simple sugars, not starches","Amylopectin is a highly branched chain of glucose (~80% of starch); amylose is a long, straight chain (~20%)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: amylopectin is highly branched (about 80% of starch) and amylose is a straight chain (about 20%). — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 8"},{"q":"According to the lecture, about how much sodium is in one teaspoon of salt, which also equals the tolerable upper limit?","choices":["About 2,300 milligrams","About 500 milligrams","About 100 milligrams","About 4,700 milligrams"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: one teaspoon of salt contains about 2,300 milligrams of sodium, equal to the tolerable upper limit. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 146"},{"q":"According to the lecture, why is vitamin K routinely given as an injection at birth?","choices":["It cannot be absorbed from formula","Infants are born with dangerously high levels","Intestinal bacteria have not yet begun to synthesize it","Breast milk contains toxic amounts of it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: vitamin K is given at birth because intestinal bacteria only begin to synthesize it afterward. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 131"},{"q":"What nutritional priority applies to trauma patients, per lecture?","choices":["Restrict protein intake substantially during the first week after injury to help minimize the body's overall metabolic stress response and inflammation.","Start nutrition support early — trauma causes a hypermetabolic state with increased calorie and protein needs for wound healing and to prevent lean muscle loss.","Delay nutrition support until the patient is fully hemodynamically stable, often waiting a week or more before starting.","Avoid enteral feeding entirely and rely instead on immediately starting parenteral nutrition delivered through a central line."],"answer":1,"exp":"Trauma patients should start nutrition support early, since trauma produces a hypermetabolic state with increased calorie and high protein needs for wound healing and preventing lean muscle loss. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is adaptive thermogenesis, per lecture?","choices":["A term commonly used as a direct, interchangeable synonym for a person's fixed basal metabolic rate measured at complete rest.","A fixed, unchanging percentage of total daily calories burned no matter the diet, environmental temperature, or activity level.","The calories burned exclusively during deep sleep cycles each night, entirely unrelated to overeating or ambient temperature.","Nonvoluntary heat production triggered by overeating or environmental temperature changes, such as fidgeting, shivering, and maintaining muscle tone/posture."],"answer":3,"exp":"Adaptive thermogenesis is nonvoluntary physical activity triggered by overeating or environmental temperature change — fidgeting, shivering, and maintaining muscle tone and posture, involving brown adipose tissue. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What percentage of adults does the lecture state are affected by irritable bowel syndrome (IBS)?","choices":["10% to 15%","1% to 5%","20% to 25%","50%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states IBS affects 10% to 15% of adults. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"According to the lecture, what proportion of young children outgrow their food allergies before age 3, and by when?","choices":["10% to 15% of young children outgrow food allergies before age 3","80% of young children outgrow food allergies before age 3","Cooking food always worsens the allergic response","Avoiding the food is rarely recommended"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states 80% of young children outgrow their food allergies before age 3. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A mother is 2 days postpartum. Which characteristics would be expected of her breastmilk at this point, per the lecture?","choices":["Thick, yellowish colostrum, rich in immunoglobulins and immune cells, with a laxative effect that aids passage of meconium.","Mature milk that is about 87% water and fully established as the primary milk supply.","Transitional milk that is far more voluminous than colostrum, typically seen 2-14 days after the birth.","Milk in which hindmilk predominates throughout, resembling cream in consistency."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes colostrum, produced during the first few days after birth, as thick and yellowish, rich in immunoglobulins and immune cells, and having a laxative effect that aids passage of newborn meconium. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"According to the lecture, which plant food provides all nine essential amino acids?","choices":["Soy products such as soy milk, tofu, and soybeans","White rice alone","Iceberg lettuce","Apples"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: soy is a plant source that provides all nine essential amino acids. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 74"},{"q":"According to the slide, which of the following is NOT listed as a nutrient deficiency concern specific to vegan diets?","choices":["Zinc","Vitamin B12","Iron","Vitamin C"],"answer":3,"exp":"The slide lists vitamin B12, vitamin D, calcium, iron, zinc, and omega-3 fatty acids as vegan diet deficiency concerns. Vitamin C is not on that list, since the fruits and vegetables that make up a larger share of a vegan diet are generally rich sources of it. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 93 (Vegan Diets)"},{"q":"According to the Food and Nutrition Board, what is the RDA for carbohydrate intake in adults?","choices":["250 grams per day","65 grams per day","130 grams per day","45 grams per day"],"answer":2,"exp":"The RDA for carbohydrate is 130 grams per day for adults — the minimum needed to supply the brain and central nervous system with adequate glucose, though average U.S. intake runs much higher (180–330 g/day). — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 35 (Carbohydrate Needs)"},{"q":"The lecture notes that excessive protein intake in older adults may pose what risk?","choices":["Reduced satiety and overeating","Increased glycemic index of meals","Accelerated decline in kidney function","Rapid loss of bone mass"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: excessive protein may accelerate decline in kidney function, though it is rare because protein is satiating. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Per the BMI guidelines presented in lecture, a BMI of 32 falls into which category?","choices":["Healthy body weight","Class 1 Obesity","Class 2 Obesity","Overweight"],"answer":1,"exp":"Class 1 Obesity spans a BMI of 30–34.99, so a BMI of 32 falls squarely in that range. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 29 (Obesity Definition — BMI Guidelines)"},{"q":"The lecture contrasts \"usual\" versus \"successful\" aging. Which best describes successful aging?","choices":["Complete prevention of any physiological change","Reliance on multiple medications to feel well","Slower age-related declines and later onset of chronic disease symptoms","Faster loss of lean body mass and steadily rising blood pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: successful aging features slower declines and delayed onset of chronic disease symptoms. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 10"}]};
